{"body": "The execution was unrunnable (proposal 2e2d5d28766bc1dc: 'the rewritten text is the input unchanged'), so there is no runtime output to compare. Reading the source beside the conjecture: the conjecture's predicted code behavior is correct. recover_json_object builds fenced candidates from the entire fenced content, not from objects inside that content. For Reply A the fenced pool is ['Here is {\"b\":2}', '{\"a\":1}']; reversed, it tries 'Here is {\"b\":2}', fails strict and last-wins parsing, then returns '{\"a\":1}' from the first fence, never reaching top_level. The documented rule says 'The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text.' The last object inside a code fence in Reply A is {\"b\":2}, because it sits inside the second fence even though the fenced content as a whole is not a JSON object. So the rule as written and the code part company; the conjecture did not misread the rule. A next conjecture should try a case where the last fence contains prose plus a JSON object and assert that recover_json_object should extract that object, or should propose changing the fenced pool to scan fenced content for top-level objects. It should avoid repeating the assumption that the fenced pool already extracts objects from within fenced content, and avoid relying on an unrunnable execution as evidence."}